\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Eliminating a positive Gaussian block}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Split a finite vertex set as #{V=R\sqcup U}, with nonempty retained
set #{R} and eliminated set #{U}. Put #{p=|R|}, #{q=|U|}, and write a
field as #{(x,y)\in\mathbb R^R\times\mathbb R^U}. Both spaces have
their counting inner products and coordinate Lebesgue measures; the
full measure is #{dx\,dy}. In this ordering, a
\vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004} has blocks
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}A&H\\H^T&C\end{pmatrix}>0.}
Here #{A} is #{p\times p}, #{C} is #{q\times q}, and #{H} is
#{p\times q}. Define the \newvocab{effective precision}, or
\newvocab{Schur complement}
of #{C}, by
##{S=A-HC^{-1}H^T.}
Then #{C>0}, #{S>0}, and integrating the unnormalized weight gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{\mathbb R^U}
 \exp\left(-\tfrac12(x,y)^TQ(x,y)\right)dy\\
&\qquad=c_C\exp\left(-\tfrac12x^TSx\right),\\
&c_C=\frac{(2\pi)^{q/2}}{\sqrt{\det C}}>0.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the operation sends #{Q} to the pair #{(S,c_C)}, not just a matrix.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5}{boyd2004convex}, pp. 650-651, gives the
square completion with the other block as pivot. Exchanging the block
order gives the formula above.
\citet{Equation (A.2)}{rasmussen2006gaussian}, p. 200, gives the
Gaussian integral used in the factor #{c_C}.}

\proof{
\p{For #{y\ne0}, positivity of #{Q} on #{(0,y)} gives
#{y^TCy>0}. Thus #{C} is positive definite and invertible. Set
#{K=C^{-1}H^T}. Expanding the right side gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&(x,y)^TQ(x,y)\\
&\quad=x^TSx\\
&\qquad+(y+Kx)^TC(y+Kx).
\end{aligned}}
Indeed #{CK=H^T} and #{K^TCK=HC^{-1}H^T}; the latter cancels the
subtracted term in #{S}. For nonzero #{x}, substitute #{y=-Kx}.
The vector #{(x,-Kx)} is nonzero, so #{x^TSx>0}. Symmetry of #{C^{-1}}
also makes #{S} symmetric. This proves #{S>0}.}

\p{In matrix form the same identity is
##{\begin{aligned}
Q&=T^T\begin{pmatrix}S&0\\0&C\end{pmatrix}T,\\
T&=\begin{pmatrix}I&0\\K&I\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det T=1.
\end{aligned}}
Taking determinants gives #{\det Q=\det C\det S}.
For each fixed #{x}, the translation #{z=y+Kx} preserves Lebesgue
measure. The [positive Gaussian integral](fdip-0005) for #{C} is
finite and equals #{c_C}; pulling out the factor depending only on
#{x} proves the displayed fiber integral. No exchange of signed
integrals is needed.}
}

\p{Taking minus the logarithm of the fiber integral defines an exact
\newvocab{effective action}
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A_{\mathrm{eff}}(x)
 &=\tfrac12x^TSx-\log c_C\\
 &=\tfrac12x^TSx+\tfrac12\log\det C\\
 &\quad-\tfrac q2\log(2\pi).
\end{aligned}}
The constant cancels from normalized retained expectations, but is
part of the unnormalized weight and its partition function. The
carrier, split, input precision and volume measure were supplied;
#{S} and #{c_C} are induced relative to these choices. This finite
operation alone does not supply the dynamical lattice or effective
metric considered in \citet{Abstract}{shamir1994dynamical}.}

\p{Singular #{C} cannot occur under #{Q>0}. If one weakens the
hypothesis to #{Q\ge0} and allows #{0\ne z\in\ker C}, positivity of
the quadratic form on #{(x,rz)} for every real #{r} forces
#{x^THz=0} for every #{x}, hence #{Hz=0}. The weight is then constant
along the eliminated direction #{z}. Orthogonal coordinates along
#{\mathbb R z} and its complement, followed by Tonelli, give an
infinite fiber integral for every #{x}. For example
#{Q=\operatorname{diag}(1,0)} gives
#{\exp(-x^2/2)\int_{\mathbb R}dy=\infty}. A pseudoinverse does
not turn this integral into a finite one.}

\p{One may instead \em{change the eliminated space} to
#{U_0=(\ker C)^\perp}, with its induced Euclidean volume. For
#{Q\ge0}, the preceding argument implies
#{H^Tx\in U_0}. On #{U_0}, #{C} is positive definite, so the same
square completion gives
##{\begin{aligned}
S_0&=A-HC^+H^T,\\
c_0&=\frac{(2\pi)^{r/2}}{\sqrt{\operatorname{pdet}C}},
\qquad r=\operatorname{rank}C.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{C^+} inverts the positive eigenvalues and is zero on the kernel;
#{\operatorname{pdet}C} is their product. In rank zero the empty
product and the volume of the zero-dimensional space are both one.
A normalized law on the retained full space still requires #{S_0>0}.
This constrained measure is a new model, as in the
[massless mean-zero construction](fdip-000C).}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.schur_complement_eq₂₂} supplies the
block quadratic identity, and \leanref{Matrix.det_fromBlocks₂₂}
supplies the determinant factorization with an invertible lower-right
block. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{Matrix.PosDef.gaussianCondCov} proves strict
positivity of this Schur complement for a positive definite block
matrix. Its name refers to a conditional-covariance application;
the algebraic conclusion applies to our precision matrix. The
Lebesgue integral and effective-action conclusions above additionally
use the stated measure and Gaussian normalization.}
